K51=C/'K51-01_transcription.tex';K512=C/'K51-02_transcription.tex';K52=C/'K52-01_transcription.tex'
cl(136,[],49,[3,4],K49,'6–58','Pic^τ_{A/S}=Pic^0_{A/S} is an abelian scheme of the same relative dimension.','For an abelian scheme A/S, its dual Pic^0_{A/S} is an abelian scheme, compatible with base change; Pic^τ=Pic^0 since the geometric Néron–Severi groups are torsion free.','proof sketched','A','FGA VI is the author’s published Picard programme; full general representability is stated in Lan Theorem1.3.2.3, with the Artin–Raynaud–Deligne completion explicitly distinguished from the manuscript’s projective case. Kleiman Remark6.22 gives Pic^τ=Pic^0.',['AG45-FGAVI','AG45-Lan2008','AG45-Kleiman2005'],date='FGA VI lectureMay1962; later general representability completed by Artin, Raynaud and Deligne, as recorded in Lan2008/revision2021; no unique first-circulation date for the full generality established.')
cl(137,[],49,[3,4],K49,'7–38','dim H¹(A₀,O)=n, hence the total cohomology structure.','For a g-dimensional abelian variety over a field, h¹(O)=g and cup product identifies H*(A,O) with Λ*H¹(A,O).','proof sketched','E','The field result predates the relative argument. Lan Proposition2.1.5.14 gives its relative extension; the modern source points to Berthelot–Breen–Messing Proposition2.5.2.',['AG45-Lan2008'],'An exterior algebra on g degree1 generators has degreei dimension binomial(g,i); this dimension deduction is separate from proving the exterior-algebra theorem.')
cl(138,[],49,[4],K49,'43–58','Flatness lemma: F or G flat; fibrewise injectivity implies G flat.','Reconstruction: under the required finite-presentation hypotheses, if F is S-flat at points over y and the map G_s→φ_(s,*)F_s is injective at y in the stated stalkwise sense, the associated-graded flatness argument is intended to prove S-flatness of coherent G at y. The transcription leaves F/G and the basepoint ambiguous.','proof sketched','F','Local flatness criteria in EGA/Stacks explain the proposed mechanism, but the exact lemma cannot be identified until the F/G reading, stalk quantifiers and quasi-coherence/finite hypotheses are fixed. No published theorem is substituted for the ambiguous statement.',['AG45-StacksFlat'],confidence='low')
cl(139,[],49,[5,6],K49,'62–87','R*f_*O_A=Λ*R¹f_*O_A; cohomological flatness.','For any abelian scheme f:A→S of relative dimension g, R¹f_*O_A is locally free of rankg, all R^if_*O_A commute with arbitrary base change, and cup product gives Λ^iR¹f_*O_A≅R^if_*O_A.','proved there','B','Lan Proposition2.1.5.14 states the exterior algebra of locally free sheaves; its cited Berthelot–Breen–Messing Proposition2.5.2 supplies the cohomology/base-change formulation. The manuscript proves the projective case using Picard smoothness.',['AG45-Lan2008','AG45-BBM1982'],'Once coherent base change gives locally free source and target, a morphism whose every residue-field fibre is an isomorphism is an isomorphism by Nakayama. The page correctly separates this last step from its cohomological-flatness argument.')
cl(140,[],49,[6],K49,'89','«J’ignore» the nonprojective version and existence/smoothness of Pic.','The dual representability and coherent cohomology assertions remain true for abelian schemes without assuming local projectivity. This does not imply that every abelian scheme is locally projective.','open on the pages','B','Lan Theorem1.3.2.3 and Proposition2.1.5.14 settle the stated assertions. Kleiman Remark5.24 records Grothendieck’s nonprojective examples, including over dual numbers in characteristic0, and cites Raynaud1966 Remark8.c.',['AG45-Lan2008','AG45-Kleiman2005','AG45-BBM1982'])
cl(141,[],49,[6],K49,'91–93','L ample iff φ_L is finite faithfully flat.','An ample line bundle induces an isogeny. The converse fails: a nondegenerate line bundle may have nonzero index, and an anti-ample bundle already induces an isogeny.','false as stated','E','Milne Theorem13.3 gives degreeφ_L=χ(L)² and the index theorem. A polarization requires positivity in addition to a symmetric isogeny, as in Lan Proposition1.3.2.15.',['AG45-MilneAV','AG45-Lan2008'],'math_checks.py:inverse_ample_isogeny. For an elliptic curve E over C, L=O_E(−0) has degree−1 and is not ample; φ_L=−φ_(O_E(0)) is an isomorphism, hence finite faithfully flat with trivial finite flat kernel. Every displayed hypothesis is satisfied.')
cl(142,[],49,[6],K49,'95–99','The ample locus Pic⁺ is open and closed.','For a line bundle on an abelian scheme over a connected base, ampleness on geometric fibres is constant; equivalently the ample locus of the relative Picard scheme is open and closed. This assertion survives even though the preceding claimed iff is false.','proof sketched','B','Robert Proposition2.3.13 states the open-and-closed polarization locus, and Lan Proposition1.3.2.15 supplies the fibrewise ample criterion. The proof must retain the index/positivity condition.',['AG45-Robert2023','AG45-Lan2008'])
cl(143,[],49,[6,7,8],K49,'101;104–145','Existence of the Néron–Severi scheme; closed map into Hom(A,A′).','The relative étale/fppf Néron–Severi sheaf is the quotient Pic/Pic⁰ and maps injectively by L↦φ_L to the sheaf of symmetric homomorphisms A→A∨. It is not asserted to be locally constant in every family.','proof sketched','A','FGA VI and Kleiman §§5–6 provide relative Picard components. Lan Proposition1.3.2.14 identifies symmetric homomorphisms étale locally with those arising from line bundles; symmetry and topology are essential.',['AG45-FGAVI','AG45-Kleiman2005','AG45-Lan2008'])
cl(144,[],49,[7,8,9,10],K49,'104–185','φ_L additive; kernel Pic⁰; translation invariance and primitive bundles.','For an abelian scheme, φ_(L⊗M)=φ_L+φ_M. A rigidified line bundle is in Pic⁰ iff its translations are fibrewise equivalent to itself, equivalently it has the primitive/cubical structure giving an extension by G_m.','proof sketched','E','The theorem of the square/cube and Barsotti–Weil theory establish these equivalences. Lan Construction1.3.2.7, Lemma1.3.2.13 and §3.2.3 specify the rigidification and cubical structure; the manuscript defers part of the field proof.',['AG45-Lan2008','AG45-Booher2015'])
cl(145,[],49,[7,8],K49,'118–145','Néron–Severi map is a closed immersion into Hom.','For projective abelian schemes with the stated representability, the symmetric-Hom subfunctor is closed and the Pic/Pic⁰ map identifies it étale locally. A literal assertion about an unspecified Néron–Severi prescheme needs the quotient convention.','proof sketched','B','Lan Proposition1.3.2.14 gives the exact local surjectivity; Hom representability/rigidity and the equation f=f∨ give the closed symmetric locus. It does not establish constancy of the resulting sheaf.',['AG45-Lan2008'],confidence='medium')
cl(146,[],50,[3],K50,'8–57','Ext¹(A,G_m)=Pic^τ(A); translation invariant and primitive are equivalent.','For an abelian scheme A, the relative fppf sheaf Ext¹(A,G_m) is represented by A∨=Pic⁰(A/S); group extensions correspond to rigidified algebraically trivial line bundles with their unique compatible group law.','proof sketched','E','Barsotti–Weil identification is recalled in Booher §2 and Lan §3.2.3. The manuscript itself attributes the extension interpretation to Barsotti, Cartier and Serre; no priority claim is inferred from its undated note.',['AG45-Booher2015','AG45-Lan2008'])
cl(147,[],50,[4],K50,'58–86','v(x+y)≥min(v(x),v(y)).','An additive valuation on a field with v(0)=∞ satisfies the ultrametric inequality; if v(x)≠v(y), equality holds.','proved there','E','Classical valuation theory, treated in Stacks Valuation Rings; this is auxiliary algebra on the reverse of the abelian pages.',['AG45-StacksValuation'],'If v(x)<v(y) and v(x+y)>v(x), then x=(x+y)−y has value strictly larger than v(x), a contradiction.')
cl(148,[],50,[4],K50,'78–91','Value of a sum with a unique minimum.','For a finite sum with exactly one summand of least valuation, the valuation of the sum equals that least valuation; cancellation can occur when the minimum repeats.','proved there','E','An immediate iterated ultrametric argument in the standard valuation-ring framework.',['AG45-StacksValuation'],'Let the unique minimum occur atx₁. All other terms have valuation>v(x₁), so their sum does also; apply the unequal-value case of CL-SA18-45147.')
cl(149,[],50,[6],K50,'146–165','Valuation, place and valuation-ring formulations.','A valuation ring R⊂K determines an ordered value group K*/R*, and its residue map is a place; conversely the finite locus of a place is a valuation ring. Equivalence means identical valuation ring, not identical chosen value-group labels.','proof sketched','E','Stacks Valuation Rings gives the equivalence and ordered-group construction.',['AG45-StacksValuation'],'The condition that x orx⁻¹ belongs toR totally orders K*/R*. Units have value0 and the nonunits ofR have positive value, so reduction toR/m is exactly the associated place.')
cl(150,[],50,[4,6],K50,'86–91;146–165','Extension of a valuation to a field extension.','Every valuation ring of a field K admits a dominating valuation ring in a field extension L/K, with the corresponding extension of valuation up to equivalence.','stated without proof','E','The valuation-extension theorem follows by a maximal local subring argument; Stacks Valuation Rings gives the existence theorem. The manuscript’s partial notation is not read as uniqueness.',['AG45-StacksValuation'])
cl(151,[],50,[5],K50,'92–145','Algebraic, torsion-algebraic and numerical equivalence for divisors on A.','For a line bundle on an abelian variety, numerical triviality, algebraic triviality, and having a torsion Néron–Severi class are equivalent; NS(A) is torsion free.','proof sketched','E','Kleiman Remark6.22 identifies Picτ=Pic⁰ for abelian varieties and points to Mumford1970 Corollary2 p178. This does not identify algebraic and numerical equivalence for arbitrary higher-codimension cycles.',['AG45-Kleiman2005','AG45-MilneAV'])
cl(152,[],50,[7,8,9],K50,'168–183;189–229;236–255','Positive intersection of an ample class with α*ξ.','For ample ξ on a g-dimensional abelian variety, (ξ^(g−1)·α*ξ)>0 for every nonzero homomorphism α:A→A. It is0 for α=0.','proof sketched','E','Milne Theorem17.3 identifies this intersection with a positive Rosati trace. The crossed-out nonzero qualification in the margin is mathematically necessary.',['AG45-MilneAV'],'A nonzero α has a positive-dimensional image; α*ξ is nef and nonzero, and intersecting with g−1 ample divisors is positive. For α=0 its pullback is trivial, so the intersection is0.')
cl(153,[],50,[8],K50,'189–229','Degree ν is a homogeneous polynomial of degree2r.','The degree function on End⁰(A), extended from isogenies, is a homogeneous polynomial of degree2g; ν(n+α) has leading term n^(2g).','proof sketched','E','Milne §12’s characteristic polynomial and degree construction give the exact homogeneity. The relative generalization requires locally constant dimension, which is fixed on these pages.',['AG45-MilneAV'],'On a scalar endomorphism[t], degree=t^(2g), and the characteristic polynomial realizes degree on the rational endomorphism algebra. Homogeneity is verified symbolically in math_checks.py:degree_coefficients.')
cl(154,[],50,[9],K50,'231–234','σ_i has degree2i; σ₁ is trace.','If ν(n+α)=Σ_i σ_i(α)n^(2g−i), then σ_i is homogeneous of degreei, not2i. In particular σ₁ is linear.','false as stated','E','The standard characteristic polynomial in Milne §12 supplies the corrected degree. The printed2i is also incompatible with the next assertion that σ₁ is a linear trace.',['AG45-MilneAV'],'math_checks.py:degree_coefficients. Substitutingα=[t] gives(n+t)^(2g), soσ₁=2gt has degree1. In generalν(n+tα)=t^(2g)ν(n/t+α); compare n-coefficients to getσ_i(tα)=t^iσ_i(α).')
cl(155,[],50,[7,9],K50,'168–183;233–255','Trace α′α equals the intersection expression.','For a polarizationλ=φ_ξ and Rosatiα†=λ⁻¹α∨λ, Tr(α†α)=2g(ξ^(g−1)·α*ξ)/(ξ^g).','proof sketched','E','Milne Theorem17.3 states this precise identity with its normalization. The manuscript’s factor r times the intersection with2α*ξ is the same2r normalization.',['AG45-MilneAV'],'Differentiate the degree identity at1, or compare the linear coefficient of the intersection polynomial; the equality of the two displayed normalizations is2g·I(ξ^(g−1),α*ξ)=g·I(ξ^(g−1),2α*ξ).')
cl(156,[],50,[7,9],K50,'168–183;236–255','The Rosati trace form is positive.','For a polarized abelian variety, the quadratic form α↦Tr(α†α) on End⁰(A) is positive definite.','proof sketched','E','Milne Theorem17.3 proves positivity by the preceding intersection formula; this is a classical polarization theorem, not a new claim of the manuscript.',['AG45-MilneAV'])
cl(157,[],50,[10],K50,'269–274','The duality is an involution satisfying (uv)′=v′u′.','A polarization defines an involutive anti-automorphism of End⁰(A) byα†=λ⁻¹α∨λ.','proof sketched','E','Milne §17 gives Rosati on the rational endomorphism algebra. Biduality andλ∨=λ prove involutivity.',['AG45-MilneAV','AG45-Lan2008'],'(αβ)∨=β∨α∨ gives order reversal. Applying the construction twice and usingλ∨=λ and canonical biduality yieldsα.')
cl(158,[],50,[10],K50,'269–274','End(A) is stable under the displayed duality.','If End(A) means integral endomorphisms, stability can fail for a nonprincipal polarization. The involution always exists onEnd⁰(A).','false as stated','E','Milne §17 explicitly works rationally. If the handwritten notation already meansEnd⊗Q, there is no mathematical error, only a reading convention to settle.',['AG45-MilneAV'],'math_checks.py:rosati_rational. For E/C withoutCM, A=E² and polarizationdiag(1,2), integralα=E₁₂ hasα†=diag(1,1/2)E₂₁diag(1,2)=(1/2)E₂₁, outsideM₂(Z).',confidence='medium')
cl(159,[],50,[10],K50,'260–263','NS(A×B)=NS(A)⊕NS(B)⊕Hom(A,B′).','For abelian varieties over an algebraically closed field, rigidification and the Poincaré bundle splitNS(A×B) into the two factorNS groups andHom(A,B∨). Over other bases use relative sheaves or specify descent.','stated without proof','E','The Poincaré universal property and theorem of the cube in Lan §1.3.2/§3.2.3 give the cross-term correspondence. The base-field restriction here is our reconstruction of an unqualified line.',['AG45-Lan2008'],'Subtract the two axis restrictions from a line bundle onA×B. The remaining bundle is trivial on both axes and gives a morphismA→Pic⁰(B)=B∨, necessarily a homomorphism by rigidity. Poincaré pullback is the inverse construction.')
cl(160,[],50,[10],K50,'278–299','Dξ=m*ξ−p*ξ−q*ξ gives the bilinear bundle.','A rigidified line bundleL onA definesD₂(L)=m*L⊗p₁*L⁻¹⊗p₂*L⁻¹; it is the Poincaré pullback along(1,φ_L), is symmetric, and is additive in each variable as a biextension.','proof sketched','E','Lan Construction1.3.2.7 and Lemma1.3.2.13 give the exact construction. The line295 target/type ambiguity is a scan-reading issue.',['AG45-Lan2008'],'The theorem of the cube trivializes the third finite difference, which is exactly additivity ofD₂(L) in each variable; factor exchange proves its symmetry.')
cl(161,[],50,[11],K50,'305–355','Abstract dual category withκ and antisymmetricN(A); skew block formula.','The formalism depends on the chosen bidualityκ. Standard abelian-variety duality makes polarization homomorphisms symmetric; a signedκ convention can instead call the corresponding class antisymmetric. The page does not fix enough signs to identifyN(A) uniquely.','proof sketched','F','Lan Lemma1.3.2.13 provides the standard symmetric convention. The manuscript’s contrary adjective cannot be silently corrected or treated as a theorem in that convention.',['AG45-Lan2008'],'ForD₂(L), exchanging the two factors leaves the bundle unchanged. Thus with standard bidualityφ_L∨=φ_L; obtaininga minus sign requires changingκ or the bilinear identification.',confidence='low')
cl(162,[],51,[3,4,5],K51,'8–93','Saturation J* of a generic-fibre submodule gives the unique flat extension.','Over a DVRR withuniformizerπ, for anR-flat moduleF and generic submoduleJ_K⊂F_K, the saturated submoduleJ*=F∩J_K has torsion-free quotientF/J*, hence flat; it is the unique quotient-flat extension ofJ_K.','proved there','E','The DVR flatness criterion is the standard valuation-ring theorem; Stacks flatness/valuation sections provide the exact framework.',['AG45-StacksFlat','AG45-StacksValuation'],'J*={x∈F:π^n x∈J for some n}; ifπx∈J* thenx∈J*, soF/J* has noπ-torsion. Conversely any quotient-flat extension isπ-saturated and equals the intersection withJ_K.')
cl(163,[],51,[5,6],K51,'69–141','Flat schematic closure of a generic subgroup is a group scheme.','For a flat group scheme over a DVR, the schematic closure of a generic closed subgroup is flat; multiplication, inversion and identity factor through it by schematic density.','proved there','A','EGA schematic closure and SGA3 VIB group-scheme constructions formalize this argument. Properness of the ambient group is not needed for closure to be a subgroup.',['AG45-SGA3VIB','AG45-StacksClosure'],'The saturated defining ideal restricts to the generic subgroup ideal. Composing multiplication with its quotient vanishes generically; flatness of the product makes it vanish globally. Inversion and identity are checked identically.')
cl(164,[],51,[4,5,6],K51,'43–141','Flat closures commute with products over the valuation ring.','For flat ambient schemes over a DVR, the product of the flat closures of two generic subschemes is flat and has the prescribed generic product, hence equals its schematic closure.','proved there','E','Schematic density and uniqueness of flat closure give the result; EGA/Stacks closure and flatness lemmas apply.',['AG45-StacksClosure','AG45-StacksFlat'],'A tensor product of flatR-algebras isflat, so its generic fibre is schematically dense. Both constructions therefore have the same generic ideal and quotient-flat saturation.')
cl(165,[],51,[7,8],K51,'142–192','The subgroup condition cuts out a closed subscheme ofHilb.','For a proper finitely presented group scheme with a Hilbert scheme of flat finitely presented subschemes, requiring containment of the identity and stability under multiplication and inversion gives a closed subfunctor, on each suitable bounded Hilbert piece.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 XI Remark6.12 supplies closedness of containment when the source is flat and proper; the cited Murre representability theorem treats the Hilbert setting.',['AG45-SGA3XI'],'Use the universal Hilbert familyH. Apply closed containment to e:S→G, inversionH→G and multiplicationH×H→G. Proper flatness ofH andH×H gives the required closed conditions.')
cl(166,[],51,[7,8],K51,'142–192','Multiplication and inversion alone express the subgroup condition.','The empty flat closed subscheme is stable under multiplication and inversion but is not a subgroup. The identity containment condition must be included, or emptiness must be excluded with a condition forcing the unit.','false as stated','E','This is an elementary correction to the displayed Hilbert condition, compatible with SGA3’s definition of a subgroup scheme. It is not a counterexample if an omitted nonemptiness/unit condition is established by the ink.',['AG45-SGA3XI'],'ForH=∅, mapsH×H→H andH→H exist uniquely, whileS→H does not for nonemptyS. Thus all two listed closure conditions hold and the unit axiom fails.',confidence='medium')
cl(167,[],51,[7,8],K51,'142–192','The subgroup parameter space is locally proper.','IfG/S is projective and finitely presented, each fixed-Hilbert-polynomial closed subgroup locus is proper overS. The union is locally of finite presentation; it need not be one finite-type proper scheme.','proof sketched','A','The Hilbert-scheme properness theorem in FGA IV, together with the closed subgroup conditions, proves this bounded formulation. The manuscript also uses uniqueness of flat closure for its valuative argument.',['AG45-FGAIV','AG45-SGA3XI'])
cl(168,[],51,[8],K51,'166–192','Finite, smooth and abelian subgroup loci.','Within a proper flat finitely presented family of subgroups, the finite locus is open (and on fixed-degree/relative-dimension pieces behaves as indicated), smoothness is open, and the smooth geometrically connected locus is the abelian subgroup locus. A blanket closedness assertion requires the Hilbert-polynomial restriction.','proof sketched','A','EGA openness and proper smooth connected-component results provide these loci. Positive-characteristic specialization can destroy smoothness, so the abelian locus is not generally proper.',['AG45-FGAIV','AG45-SGA3VIB','AG45-StacksSmooth'],confidence='medium')
cl(169,[],51,[9,10,11],K51,'193–253','Good reduction is invariant under isogeny.','For abelian varieties over the fraction field of a DVR, an isogeny preserves good reduction in both directions.','proof sketched','E','Koizumi–Shimura1959 is expressly cited on the manuscript; the modern Néron–Ogg–Shafarevich criterion gives the result immediately. Robert Theorem3.1.10 and its corollary state the exact isogeny consequence.',['AG45-KS1959','AG45-Robert2023','AG45-Neron1964'])
cl(170,[],51,[9,10,16],K51,'193–218', 'A generic homomorphism between abelian schemes over a DVR extends uniquely.','For abelian schemesA,B over a DVRR, restrictionHom_R(A,B)→Hom_K(A_K,B_K) is an isomorphism.','proof sketched','A','The Néron mapping property proves this; Robert Example3.1.8 and Proposition3.1.9 give exact locators, referring to BLR1.2.8 and1.4.4.',['AG45-Robert2023','AG45-Neron1964'],'Uniqueness follows from separatedness and schematic density ofA_K; existence applies the Néron mapping property ofB to the smoothR-schemeA. Additivity extends from the dense generic fibre.')
cl(171,[],51,[11],K51,'219–253','Subvarieties and quotients of a good-reduction abelian variety have good reduction.','For an exact sequence0→B_K→A_K→C_K→0 over a DVR fraction field, A_K has good reduction iffB_K andC_K do.','proof sketched','E','Robert Theorem3.1.10 and its exact-sequence corollary state this, via theℓ-adic criterion; the manuscript also invokes Koizumi–Shimura and Poincaré reducibility.',['AG45-KS1959','AG45-Robert2023','AG45-MilneAV'],'Poincaré complete reducibility makesA_K isogenous toB_K×C_K. CombineCL-SA18-45169 with good reduction of a product. The projector identity on the page should beq²=q after division by its nonzero integer denominator.')
cl(172,[],51,[12,13],K51,'254–279','The flat closure of an abelian generic subgroup need not be smooth.','A generic closed immersionB_K→A_K of good-reduction abelian varieties may extend to a map of abelian schemes with nontrivial special kernel; consequently the flat schematic image/closure can fail to be an abelian subscheme.','proof sketched','B','The manuscript gives finite-subgroup collision constructions, consistent with the modern distinction between extending a homomorphism and extending a closed immersion. Robert §3.1.5 only guarantees extension, not a constant-rank kernel.',['AG45-Robert2023','AG45-SGA3VIIA'],'The explicit equal-characteristic construction inCL-SA18-45173 verifies the mechanism, rather than assuming the uncertain mixed-characteristic elliptic example.')
cl(173,[],51,[13],K512,'4–21','Move two Lie lines with zero p-map until their finite subgroup schemes meet.','Overk[[t]] withk algebraically closed ofcharacteristicp, take a supersingular elliptic curveE andB=E². InsideB[F]≅α_p² takeΓ₁={(u,0)} andΓ₂={(u,tu)}. Their generic intersection is trivial and their special fibres coincide.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 VIIA §7 identifies these height-one groups with restricted Lie algebras. The exact Hopf construction validates the author’s corrected equal-characteristic example without relying on the unresolved wording.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA'],'math_checks.py:moving_alpha_p. BothΓ_i have coordinate algebraR[u]/u^p and are finite flat ofrankp. OverK,tu=0 impliesu=0; overk,t=0 givesΓ₁=Γ₂. The diagonalB→B/Γ₁×B/Γ₂ has kernelΓ₁∩Γ₂, so it is generically injective but not on the special fibre.')
cl(174,[],51,[14],K512,'40–43','All closed subgroup schemes ofA are locally unramified in the parameter space, in every characteristic.','The literal all-subgroups statement is false in characteristicp. The family ofα_p-lines in a superspecial abelian surface is parameterized byP¹, hence has nonzero tangent directions. Rigidity for homomorphisms or smooth abelian subgroups is a different statement.','false as stated','A','SGA3 VIIA height-one group theory supplies the exact counterexample; this must not be replaced by Hom-scheme rigidity. The immediately preceding manuscript example already points to the failure.',['AG45-SGA3VIIA','AG45-Groth1966Hom'],'Using the chartΓ_t={(u,tu)} inα_p², reduce toR=k[ε]/ε² andt=ε. The ideals(y) and(y−εx) inR[x,y]/(x^p,y^p) are distinct, sinceεx≠0. Thus a fixed subgroup has a nontrivial infinitesimal deformation. Every subgroup is closed and finite flat inside the constant abelian surfaceE².')
cl(175,[],51,[14,15],K512,'28–68','Characteristic0 generic subgroups extend over normalS, with a unibranch variant.','For a normal noetherian characteristic0 base and an abelian scheme with the stated Hilbert representability, a generic closed subgroup extends uniquely to a flat smooth proper subgroup with the same components; connected generic fibre gives an abelian subscheme. The proposed merely reduced-base replacement is left as a question.','proof sketched','A','The published Hom-scheme extension/rigidity framework and relative subgroup schemes establish the normal-base setting; SGA3 VIII/X and Grothendieck1966 distinguish multiplicative/abelian rigidity. The reduced/unibranch modification needs its precise finiteness and geometric-unibranch hypotheses.',['AG45-Groth1966Hom','AG45-SGA3XI'],confidence='medium')
cl(176,[],51,[16],K512,'86–98','A finite unramified birational map over an unibranch base is an isomorphism.','For an integral geometrically unibranch noetherianX, an integral finite unramified birationalX′→X is an isomorphism. For a rational section of a general finite unramified map, apply this to its scheme-theoretic image, with reducedness controlled.','proof sketched','A','EGAIV18.10 and the Stacks unramified/radicial criteria give the refined statement. Ordinary unibranch versus geometrically unibranch and nilpotents must not be silently conflated.',['AG45-EGAIV4','AG45-StacksUnramified'],'Geometric unibranchness forces the finite birational map to be universally injective; unramified plus universal injectivity is a locally closed immersion. Being finite gives a closed immersion, and injectivity of the coordinate-ring map for integral dominant morphisms forces its ideal to be0.',confidence='medium')
cl(177,[],51,[16],K512,'77–84','Hom of abelian schemes is locally nonramified and locally proper.','The Hom functor between abelian schemes is represented by a separated unramified scheme locally of finite presentation; bounded pieces satisfy the valuative extension property. This does not assert thatHom is globally finite or proper.','open on the pages','A','Grothendieck1966, Un théorème sur les homomorphismes de schémas abéliens, is the direct published descendant; Lan’s Hom/rigidity arguments and Néron extension give the modern formulation.',['AG45-Groth1966Hom','AG45-Lan2008','AG45-Robert2023'])
cl(178,[],51,[17],K512,'101–125','A projective family simple in codimension≤1 generatingA_K forces good reduction.','Under the written flatness/geometric-integrality and fibrewise smoothness-in-codimension1 assumptions over a DVR, the proposed proof cuts a smooth relative curve whose Jacobian surjects ontoA_K. Existence of that relative curve, including finite residue fields and base extension/descent, is an essential missing step.','proof sketched','C','Koizumi’s specialization theorem and modern good-reduction criteria explain the intended conclusion; no opened exact theorem was matched to every handwrittenR₁/flatness qualifier. The smooth-projective version is established, but the stated singular version requires a separate Bertini check.',['AG45-Koizumi1960','AG45-Robert2023'],confidence='medium')
cl(179,[],51,[18],K512,'127–153','Albanese and reduced Pic⁰ have good reduction.','For the stated projective DVR family with geometrically integral fibres smooth in codimension1, the author aims to applyCL-SA18-45178 to the Albanese; duality gives the reduced Picard variety. The reduction qualifier matters in characteristicp.','proof sketched','C','Koizumi1960 is the explicit historical comparator. Modern Picard theory confirms the smooth proper case, while the exact weakened singular hypotheses were not matched to an opened complete proof.',['AG45-Koizumi1960','AG45-Kleiman2005','AG45-Robert2023'],confidence='medium')
cl(180,[],51,[19,20,21,22],K512,'154–269','A unique relative abelian subscheme underlyingPic⁰; proof only incharacteristic0.','For a smooth proper family in characteristic0, Pic⁰ is an abelian scheme. The proposed extension to families merely smooth in codimension1 uses unproved constancy ofPicard dimension and elimination of a finite kernel; the margin explicitly limits the proof tocharacteristic0.','proof sketched','C','FGA VI and Kleiman’s Picard-scheme theorems establish the smooth proper case. They do not by themselves prove the manuscript’s broader singular-fibre formulation or an arbitrary-characteristic reduced identity component.',['AG45-FGAVI','AG45-Kleiman2005'],confidence='medium')
cl(181,[],51,[22],K512,'244–269','The remaining special kernel is a p-group; it vanishes ifp=1.','In the proposed extensionu:A→Pic⁰, the finite special kernel has no prime-to-p torsion under the stated nonramification ofPic[n]; hence it is p-primary, and trivial in residue characteristic0. This reduction does not eliminate the infinitesimalp-primary kernel.','proof sketched','A','Étale prime-to-p torsion and Picard rigidity in FGA/SGA give this reduction. The manuscript correctly stops before the characteristicp conclusion.',['AG45-FGAVI','AG45-SGA3VIII','AG45-Kleiman2005'],'After strict henselization, A[n] for(n,p)=1 is constant finite étale. A section mapped to0 in the special fibre of an unramified target must agree with the zero section on the connected base. Generic triviality then excludes that section. Any nontrivial prime-to-p part of a finite group scheme over an algebraically closed field would supply such a section.')
cl(182,[],51,[19],K512,'170–180','Picτ proper? Pic a disjoint union of proper opens?','Properness ofPicτ holds under suitable proper flat geometrically normal/integral hypotheses; a disjoint union into proper open pieces depends on the base and component specialization. The page asks these questions and expressly warns about formal-only families.','open on the pages','C','Kleiman §§5–6 and FGA VI give finite-type/properness criteria with explicit hypotheses. They do not justify a universal statement for all projective families with the crossed-out assumptions.',['AG45-Kleiman2005','AG45-FGAVI'],confidence='medium')
cl(183,[],51,[22],K512,'271–274','Study kernels and images of homomorphisms over nonreduced bases.','A general kernel of a homomorphism of abelian schemes need not be flat, and its image need not be an abelian subscheme. Constant-rank/flat-kernel hypotheses yield a quotient abelian scheme; infinitesimal base change requires those hypotheses to persist.','open on the pages','C','Lan’s isogeny results and Robert Proposition2.3.15 give the finite-flat/quotient cases; CL-SA18-45173 supplies the rank-jumping obstruction. The manuscript poses a programme rather than a single unqualified theorem.',['AG45-Lan2008','AG45-Robert2023'],confidence='medium')
cl(184,[],51,[23],K512,'276–283','A preabelian scheme over regularS is projective, with an isotrivial translation torsor.','The assertion requires the page’s meaning ofpreabelian and whetherprojective means locally projective. A proper smooth genus1/abelian torsor has a translation abelian scheme under suitable hypotheses, but global projectivity is stronger and cannot be inferred from regularity alone without the precise finiteness/polarization setting.','stated without proof','F','Raynaud’s theory of ample line bundles and torsors, summarized in Kleiman Remark5.24 and Robert §2.3, distinguishes local projectivity, global projectivity and representability. The manuscript’s unexpandedpreabelian/isotrivial terminology prevents an exact identification.',['AG45-Raynaud1970','AG45-Kleiman2005','AG45-Robert2023'],confidence='low')
cl(185,[],51,[23],K512,'285–289','H¹(S,G) injects intoH¹(K,G) forregularS.','For an integral regular base and an abelian schemeG, aG-torsor trivial on the generic point is trivial whenever rational sections of the torsor extend by the abelian extension theorem; in the standard noetherian setting this gives injectivity.','stated without proof','B','The rational-map extension theorem for abelian schemes is recorded in Robert Proposition2.3.16, citing BLR8.4.6. Applying it after an étale trivialization and descending the unique extension gives the torsor statement.',['AG45-Robert2023','AG45-Raynaud1970'],'On an étale cover trivializing the torsor, a generic section is a rational map toG. Regularity is preserved étale locally; extend it uniquely and use uniqueness on overlaps to descend. No existence of a new abelian model is asserted.')
cl(186,[],51,[23],K512,'291–306','The bad locus for a torsor is pure codimension1; local obstruction groups.','For a fixed abelian schemeG over an appropriate regular noetherian base, the author expects aG_K-torsor extending at all codimension1 points to extend globally. This purity question must be distinguished from purity for extendingG itself.','open on the pages','C','Abelian torsor purity is studied in Raynaud’s theory; the exact generality of the displayed direct-sum injection and its completion invariance was not established by the opened sources. The Vasiu–Zink counterexamples for extending abelian schemes alone do not refute this fixed-G torsor assertion.',['AG45-Raynaud1970','AG45-VasiuZink2010'],confidence='medium')
cl(187,[],51,[23],K512,'308–309','Analogous purity theorem for good reduction of abelian schemes?','Unrestricted extension of abelian schemes from the complement of codimension≥2 in every regular base is false in mixed characteristic. Positive results require healthy/quasi-healthy regularity or explicit ramification hypotheses.','open on the pages','B','Vasiu–Zink Theorem3/Corollaries4–5 give positive extension criteria andTheorem28 gives counterexamples. Their introduction identifies the regular local ringW(k)[[T₁,T₂]]/(p−(T₁T₂)^(p−1)) as a failure of the unrestricted assertion.',['AG45-VasiuZink2010'],date='Opened preprint dated30October2009; publishedDocumentaMath15(2010),571–599; distinct first posting not established.')
cl(188,[],52,[3,4],K52,'8–44','Aut(C)→Aut(J) is faithful, proper and unramified for genus≥2.','For a smooth proper geometrically connected curveC ofgenusg≥2, the natural map of automorphism functors to its principally polarized Jacobian is a closed immersion; the induced automorphism determines the curve automorphism.','proof sketched','E','Classical Torelli and the faithful Jacobian action establish the field statement; Ricolfi §2 records the family Torelli framework and its marked-level version. The page’s displayed Lefschetz sign is checked separately.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020','AG45-MilneJac'])
cl(189,[],52,[4],K52,'27–44','Lefschetz count is corrected to2g−2.','If an automorphism ofC acts trivially onH¹, its Lefschetz number is2−2g. For a nonidentity automorphism of a smooth proper curve this is incompatible with the nonnegative graph-diagonal intersection. The displayed2g−2 reverses the needed sign.','false as stated','E','The trace formula gives1−Tr(H¹)+1=2−2g; classical faithful-action proofs use this contradiction. The alternative reading is a scan question, not an emendation.',['AG45-MilneJac'],'The exact cohomological sum is1−2g+1. Sinceg≥2 it is negative. If the graph differs from the diagonal, their intersection multiplicities onC×C are nonnegative, including possible wild multiplicities; thus identity action forces the automorphism to beidentity.')
cl(190,[],52,[4],K52,'27–44','A translation preserving the Abel–Jacobi curve is trivial.','For an Abel–Jacobi embedding of a genus≥2 curve in its Jacobian, no nonzero translation preserves its image.','proof sketched','E','Ricolfi §1 uses the translation orbitJ inside the Hilbert scheme; faithfulness of the induced Jacobian action gives the trivial stabilizer.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020','AG45-MilneJac'],'Iftranslationt_a preservesC, it induces an automorphismf ofC acting trivially onPic⁰(C), because translations act trivially onPic⁰(J). ByCL-SA18-45188 f=1; evaluatingi(f(x))=i(x)+a givesa=0.')
cl(191,[],52,[5],K52,'45–58','Isom of curves embeds closedly inIsom of polarized Jacobians.','For smooth proper curves ofgenus≥2 over a base, the map from curve isomorphisms to isomorphisms of their canonically principally polarized Jacobians is a closed immersion, with the usual relative representability hypotheses.','proof sketched','E','The strong Torelli theorem determines the image up to the sign involution; the marked family construction is discussed in Ricolfi §2. This is distinct from surjectivity onto all polarized-Jacobian isomorphisms.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020','AG45-MilneJac'])
cl(192,[],52,[6,7],K52,'59–91','The marked Torelli fibre is radicial; later corrected toat most2.','The first radicial assertion is withdrawn. For a fixed polarized Jacobian, marking maps differing by−1 need not come from the same curve automorphism; a nonhyperelliptic geometric fibre has two sign-related points, whereas the hyperelliptic sign is realized by its involution.','corrected by himself later','B','Oort–Steenbrink’s local Torelli quotient and Ricolfi §2 make the sign quotient explicit incharacteristic≠2. The withdrawn assertion is not retained as a theorem.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'])
cl(193,[],52,[7],K52,'80–91','At mosttwo geometric points; involutionφ↦−φ.','In the characteristic≠2 marked-level Torelli setting, the geometric fibres consist of at mosttwo sign-related markings; they merge at a hyperelliptic curve. The precise level/functor convention is part of the statement.','stated without proof','B','Ricolfi Theorem2.4 uses Oort–Steenbrink Theorem3.1 to identify the relevant quotient byΣ. It does not certify an unqualified characteristic2 version.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'],confidence='medium')
cl(194,[],52,[8],K52,'92–101','The curve Hilbert space modulo translations is the marked Torelli fibre.','For an Abel–Jacobi curve in a fixed Jacobian, quotienting its connected Hilbert component by the free translation action removes the Jacobian factor and leaves the infinitesimal marked Torelli fibre, under the chosen normalization/level hypotheses.','proof sketched','B','Ricolfi Theorem3.6 computes the Hilbert component asJ×R_g and §3.2 identifiesR_g as the scheme-theoretic Torelli fibre incharacteristic≠2.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'])
cl(195,[],52,[9,10],K52,'102–131','The infinitesimal Torelli differential is dual to multiplication of canonical forms.','The codifferential of the Torelli map atC isSym²H⁰(C,K_C)→H⁰(C,K_C²), multiplication; its cokernel is dual to the kernel of the differential.','proof sketched','B','Ricolfi §1 recalls the canonical multiplication description and its tangent-space consequences; local Torelli theory gives the exact map. The manuscript’s notation must retain the symmetric rather than unrestricted tensor quotient.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'],'Serre duality identifies the tangent space tocurve moduli withH¹(T_C) and its dual withH⁰(K_C²); the variation of the principal polarization identifies the target cotangent space withSym²H⁰(K_C). The induced map is multiplication.')
cl(196,[],52,[9,10],K52,'102–131','For nonhyperellipticC the multiplication map is surjective.','For a smooth nonhyperelliptic curve ofgenus≥3, the canonical multiplicationSym²H⁰(K_C)→H⁰(K_C²) is surjective (MaxNoether), so the Torelli differential is injective.','stated without proof','E','Ricolfi Theorem1.1 and its cited canonical multiplication criterion give the exact nonhyperelliptic conclusion. Characteristic-specific statements are kept within the opened paper’s assumptions.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'])
cl(197,[],52,[9,10],K52,'102–131','Hyperelliptic cokernel has dimension1.','For a hyperelliptic curve ofgenusg≥2 incharacteristic≠2, the canonical multiplication cokernel has dimensiong−2. It is1 only forg=3.','false as stated','B','Ricolfi Theorem1.1 gives the corresponding Hilbert tangent dimension2g−2; Theorem3.6 identifies the full infinitesimal fibre.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'],'math_checks.py:hyperelliptic_multiplication. Forπ:C→P¹, K_C=π*O(g−1), andπ_*O_C=O⊕O(−g−1). ThusH⁰(K²)=H⁰(O(2g−2))⊕H⁰(O(g−3)); products fill the first summand ofdimension2g−1, leavingg−2. This is a universal proof, with integer checks forg=2,…,8.')
cl(198,[],52,[9,10],K52,'102–131','Hilbert tangent dimension2g−2 at a hyperelliptic Abel–Jacobi curve.','For a hyperelliptic curveC embedded inJ(C), the Hilbert tangent space has dimension2g−2, decomposing into theg translation directions andg−2 additional Torelli directions.','proof sketched','B','Ricolfi Theorem1.1 explicitly states the tangent dimension, including the genus2 equality withg.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'],'The normal-bundle sequence and the Torelli differential giveh⁰(N)=g+dimker(dτ)=g+(g−2)=2g−2; math_checks.py records the dimensions.')
cl(199,[],52,[6,9,10],K52,'59–79;102–131','The hyperelliptic fibre isSpec k[ε]/ε².','In characteristic≠2, the scheme-theoretic fibre at a hyperelliptic curve isR_g=Spec k[s₁,…,s_(g−2)]/(s₁,…,s_(g−2))². It is a reduced point forg=2 and dual numbers forg=3.','false as stated','B','Ricolfi Theorem3.6 and §3.2 establish this exact nonreduced structure. Merely knowing the tangent dimension would not determine the square-zero equations.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'],date='arXiv first23April2018; openedv3 dated12February2020; publishedAlgebraNumberTheory14(6)(2020),1381–1397.')
cl(200,[],52,[10,11,12,13],K52,'114–166','Good reduction of a curve when generic and special polarized Jacobians are Jacobians.','For a genus≥2 curve over a DVR fraction field, good reduction of its principally polarized Jacobian plus a smooth-Jacobian special fibre gives the intended smooth-curve extension under the standard Torelli/descent hypotheses. Good reduction of the unpolarized Jacobian alone is insufficient.','proof sketched','B','Stable reduction identifies good Jacobian reduction with compact-type reduction; an irreducible principally polarized Jacobian special fibre excludes a nontrivial product of positive-genus components. Ricolfi Theorem1.2 gives the minimal-class ingredient; modern curve-reduction references in Robert §3.2 supply the stable-reduction formulation.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020','AG45-Robert2023'],confidence='medium')
cl(201,[],52,[12],K52,'144–156','A generating minimal-class curve on an indecomposable principally polarized variety is a Jacobian curve.','The Matsusaka–Ran criterion: an effective generating1-cycle of minimal theta degree on a principally polarized abelian variety forces a product of Jacobians; indecomposability yields a single Jacobian and an Abel–Jacobi curve. Precise multiplicities and principal polarization must be included.','stated without proof','B','Ricolfi Theorem1.2 states the minimal-class characterization used to identify Hilbert components; the manuscript’s short assertion is reconstructed with these positivity/indecomposability hypotheses.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020'])
cl(202,[],52,[13],K52,'157–166','Closure of the Jacobian locus with irreducible theta remainsJacobian.','Within the locus of principally polarized abelian varieties with indecomposable/irreducible theta, the closure of smooth Jacobians introduces no new decomposable compact-type products; precise moduli-stack and characteristic hypotheses are required.','proof sketched','B','Stable curve compactification and its compact-type Torelli map give the modern formulation, as summarized in Robert §3.2 and the Torelli discussion. This is not a claim that the Jacobian locus is closed in all ofA_g.',['AG45-Robert2023','AG45-Ricolfi2020'],confidence='medium')
cl(203,[],52,[14],K52,'167–179','Corollary2: a unique global marked curve; «this cannot be true as it stands».','The unqualified unique global marked-curve reconstruction is withdrawn on the page. Strong Torelli reconstructs the curve geometrically, but a prescribed polarized-Jacobian isomorphism may differ from a curve-induced map by−1, creating a sign descent obstruction.','corrected by himself later','B','Modern strong/local Torelli keeps the sign involution; Ricolfi §2 gives its explicit quotient. The author’s own warning must remain visible in the dossier.',['AG45-Ricolfi2020','AG45-MilneJac'],'OverC choose any nonhyperelliptic curveg≥3. If−1 onJ(C) came from a curve automorphismf, strong Torelli/faithfulness would force the hyperelliptic involution criterion, impossible for thisC. Thus the marked lift cannot exist for every prescribed sign.')
